   282	```
   283	
   284	- Each language is written **in itself**, never "Spanish".
   285	- English is preselected. First launch may *suggest* the device language by
   286	  scrolling it into view, but never auto-applies it — the owner asked for
   287	  English as the default and a silently-Spanish first launch is worse than a
   288	  tap.
   289	- Selecting a language re-renders the screen immediately, so the choice is
   290	  self-demonstrating.
   291	- The panel collapses to a single tappable row showing the current language;
   292	  tapping expands the list. On the phone layout this keeps the screen from
   293	  growing a seventh scroll section.
   294	
   295	**Secondary placement: Settings.** `SettingsOverlay` gets the same control, so
   296	the choice is changeable mid-career and is not a one-time trap.
   297	
   298	**Persistence.** A `language: Locale` field on `AppPreferences`, defaulting to
   299	`'en'`. `PREFERENCES_SCHEMA_VERSION` in
   300	[`preferences-repository.ts:13`](src/persistence/preferences-repository.ts) is
   301	**already 9**, so this bumps it to **10** and adds
   302	`LANGUAGE_PREFERENCES_SCHEMA_VERSION = 9` to the migration ladder alongside the
   303	eight existing constants, defaulting `language` to `'en'` on every older row.
   304	Note that `PreferencesSchema` is a `z.strictObject` — a new field without the
   305	matching schema entry fails validation outright rather than being ignored.
   306	
   307	It is a device preference, not a career preference — one player, one language,
   308	across careers.
   309	
   310	---
   311	
   312	## 6. Measured copy surface
   313	
   314	Counted from the tree, deduplicated per file, excluding tests, the dev harness,
   315	and `src/audit/`. These are candidate strings — the extraction pass in Phase 1
   316	will prune developer-only text — so treat them as an upper bound with roughly
   317	25% noise.
   318	
   319	| Area | Files | Strings | Words |
   320	| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
   321	| Weekly loop (shell, home, training, market, finances, table) | 20 | 905 | 5,616 |
   322	| Match (render ring, HUD, substitutions, power cut-ins) | 59 | 358 | 1,954 |
   323	| Season and ceremony (awards, celebrations, awakening, story) | 38 | 285 | 1,666 |
   324	| Onboarding and creation | 6 | 101 | 1,803 |
   325	| Settings and meta (glossary, privacy, hall of fame) | 5 | 47 | 187 |
   326	| Unclassified — `App.tsx`, overlays, walk-ons, view-models | 119 | 1,966 | 11,082 |
   327	| **TS/TSX total** | **247** | **3,662** | **22,308** |
   328	
   329	| Content file | Strings | Words |
   330	| --- | ---: | ---: |
   331	| `events.json` | 400 | 3,825 |
   332	| `assistant-guide.json` (Bert) | 223 | 1,610 |
   333	| `fulltime-coach-lines.json` | 180 | 1,408 |
   334	| `glossary.json` | 86 | 1,158 |
   335	| `onboarding.json` | 110 | 919 |
   336	| `tips.json` | 36 | 631 |
   337	| `award-ceremony-lines.json` | 60 | 542 |
   338	| `player-requests.json`, `powers.json`, `sponsors.json`, `training.json`, `fulltime-blame-lines.json` | 175 | 936 |
   339	| `clubs.json` — **names, not translated** | 170 | 340 |
   340	| **Content total (translatable)** | **1,270** | **11,029** |
   341	
   342	**Roughly 2,500–3,000 translatable strings and ~25,000 English words**, which
   343	is ~150,000 words across six target languages before the quality passes in §8.
   344	That number is the reason for phasing, and it is the number to sanity-check
   345	after Phase 2 — if Spanish costs materially more than a sixth of the budget,
   346	the scope conversation happens then, not at the end.
   347	
   348	The `unclassified` row is the one to watch: `App.tsx` alone is 2,944 lines and
   349	holds a large share of it. Splitting player-facing copy out of `App.tsx` is a
   350	natural part of Phase 1 rather than a separate refactor.
   351	
   352	---
   353	
   354	## 7. Phasing
   355	
   356	Every phase ships something that works. Nothing is left half-English at a
   357	stopping point.
   358	
   359	**Phase 0 — spikes.** Confirm the `vars()` font swap works on iOS native.
   360	Confirm VT323 renders Vietnamese legibly at the game's sizes. Both are
   361	throwaway branches; both gate the plan.
   362	
   363	**Phase 1 — plumbing, English only.** Catalog format, zod schema, `useCopy()`,
   364	the `language` preference, the picker on both screens, the CI gates. The
   365	English catalog is populated by extraction. At the end of this phase the game
   366	looks identical and behaves identically, but every string flows through the
   367	lookup. This is the phase that must not regress anything.
   368	
   369	**Phase 2 — first language end-to-end (Spanish).** Proves the pipeline, the
   370	character budget, and the layout fixes against a real translation before five
   371	more are committed. Spanish because it is the highest-value and a middling
   372	expansion factor — if Spanish fits, French and Portuguese will.
   373	
   374	**Phase 3 — Vietnamese.** Second, not last, because it is the one that can fail
   375	on font grounds. Failing early is cheaper.
   376	
   377	**Phase 4 — Portuguese, French, German, Indonesian.** German last within this
   378	group; it is the longest and will surface any remaining layout ceilings.
   379	
   380	**Phase 5 — long tail.** Events, tips, glossary, ceremony lines, coach and
   381	blame lines, Bert's full script. Largest word count, lowest per-string risk.
   382	
   383	Within each translation phase, work goes screen by screen so a partial phase is
   384	still coherent.
   385	
   386	**Every translation phase is: lock the glossary (§8.1) → translate → independent
   387	per-string review (§8.2) → blind back-translation (§8.3) → in-context spot-check
   388	(§8.4).** A phase is not done when the strings exist; it is done when every
   389	string in it carries an `ok` verdict. Phase 2 runs this loop on Spanish first
   390	precisely so the cost of the quality pass is measured on one language before six
   391	are committed to it.
   392	
   393	---
   394	
   395	## 8. Testing and CI
   396	
   397	The balance harness and golden-replay conventions already establish that this
   398	project gates on assertions rather than eyeballs. Localisation gets the same:
   399	
   400	1. **No missing keys.** Every key used by `t()` exists in the English catalog;
   401	   every English key exists in all six others. Fails the build.
   402	2. **No orphan keys.** A key in a catalog that nothing references is a failure,
   403	   not a warning — it means copy moved and the translation did not.
   404	3. **Character budget.** Every string in every locale is within its §1 ceiling.
   405	4. **Placeholder parity.** A translated string uses exactly the placeholders its
   406	   English source uses. A dropped `{player}` is a silent content bug.
   407	5. **Glyph coverage.** Every character in a locale's catalog exists in that
   408	   locale's font, checked against the TTF `cmap` — the same measurement
   409	   technique already used for column advances. This is what catches a stray `ı`
   410	   or a smart quote that Silkscreen does not have.
   411	6. **No hardcoded prose.** A lint rule over `src/ui/` and `App.tsx` rejecting
   412	   JSX text nodes and prose string literals outside the catalog. Scoped to
   413	   player-facing files; developer-mode and QA-harness strings are exempt and
   414	   stay English.
   415	7. **Golden replay unaffected.** Localisation must not touch `ENGINE_VERSION`.
   416	   If a sim change is needed to remove a sentence from `src/sim/`, that is a
   417	   version decision and gets called out, not slipped in.
   418	8. **Snapshot per locale** for the two measured-width tables, so a translation
   419	   that would clip fails in Jest rather than on Joe's phone.
   420	
   421	Device QA per language: onboarding through first match, the league table, the
   422	squad register, the financial report, and Settings — the five screens where
   423	length and glyph coverage bite.
   424	
   425	---
   426	
   427	## 9. Translation quality
   428	
   429	The CI gates in §7 prove a translation is *present, sized, and renderable*.
   430	None of them prove it is *good*. A string can pass every check and still read
   431	like a manual, invert a meaning, or call a goalkeeper the wrong thing on
   432	alternating screens. Quality needs its own pass, and every string gets one.
   433	
   434	### 8.1 Terminology is locked before translation starts
   435	
   436	The single largest quality lever across 2,000 strings is consistency, and
   437	consistency cannot be recovered after the fact. Before any phase-2+ translation
   438	begins, each language gets a **term glossary**: roughly 80 entries covering
   439	football vocabulary (goalkeeper, centre-back, clean sheet, matchday, transfer
   440	fee, wages, squad, fixture), the game's own coined nouns (Heat, the Zone, Hero
   441	License, Awakening, Training Points, Fan Shop, Buzz), and the recurring UI verbs
   442	(sign, release, license, swap, advance, build).
   443	
   444	It lives at `content/i18n/glossary/<locale>.json`, is decided once with reasons
   445	recorded, and is machine-checked: if the glossary says `es` renders "Hero
   446	License" as "Licencia de Héroe", CI fails any Spanish string that renders it
   447	another way. The game's coined terms are the ones players learn, so an
   448	inconsistent one is worse than an awkward one.
   449	
   450	### 8.2 Every string is audited at least once, by a different reviewer
   451	
   452	No string ships on the word of whoever wrote it. Each translated string passes
   453	a review by an independent reviewer — a different model, prompted as a native
   454	speaker of that language who follows football and plays management games, and
   455	given the target string, the English source, the glossary, and the screen it
   456	appears on.
   457	
   458	The reviewer returns a verdict per string:
   459	
   460	| Verdict | Meaning | Action |
   461	| --- | --- | --- |
   462	| `ok` | Ships as written | none |
   463	| `stiff` | Correct but reads written, not spoken | rewrite, re-review |
   464	| `wrong` | Meaning changed, term off-glossary, or placeholder misused | rewrite, re-review |
   465	| `long` | Correct but will not fit its cell | shorten in-language, re-review |
   466	
   467	Anything not `ok` is rewritten and goes back through review. Verdicts are
   468	recorded per string in `content/i18n/review/<locale>.json` alongside the
   469	reviewer's one-line reason, so the audit is inspectable rather than a claim —
   470	and so a later copy change to the English source can invalidate exactly the
   471	strings it affects rather than the whole language.
   472	
   473	Coverage is a CI gate of its own: **no locale ships with a string lacking an
   474	`ok` verdict against the current English source hash.** That is what makes
   475	"audited at least once" enforceable instead of aspirational.
   476	
   477	### 8.3 Blind back-translation catches meaning drift
   478	
   479	Review by a fluent reader is good at register and bad at inversions — a
   480	confident, natural sentence that says the opposite of the source reads fine.
   481	So a second, cheap check runs over the whole catalog: a reviewer that sees
   482	**only the translated string**, with no access to the English, writes what it
   483	means in English. That back-translation is diffed against the source.
   484	
   485	This is where dropped negations, swapped subjects ("you sign him" → "he signs
   486	you"), and mistranslated football idiom surface. Flagged strings go to the §8.2
   487	loop. It is run per locale after translation and again before release.
   488	
   489	### 8.4 Register spot-check, in context
   490	
   491	Twenty strings per language, sampled across the screens where voice matters
   492	most — Bert's lines, button labels, the post-match report, event prose — are
   493	reviewed **on a screenshot of the actual screen**, not in a list. Out of
   494	context, "Free" is a fine translation; on the transfer screen it should mean
   495	"available", and only the screenshot shows that.
   496	
   497	This is the pass that catches what the automated gates structurally cannot, and
   498	it is deliberately small enough to actually happen for all six languages.
   499	
   500	### 8.5 What this does not claim
   501	
   502	None of the above is a native-speaker sign-off, and the spec should not pretend
   503	otherwise. It is a layered machine audit: locked terminology, an independent
   504	per-string review, a blind back-translation, and an in-context sample. That
   505	catches the failure modes that make a translation embarrassing. It will not
   506	catch every regionalism, and the honest plan is to ship, then fix on player
   507	reports — which the catalog format makes a one-line change rather than a code
   508	edit.
   509	
   510	---
   511	
   512	## 10. Out of scope
   513	
   514	- Arabic, Chinese, and any other non-Latin script. Named in §2 with reasons.
   515	- Right-to-left layout support.
   516	- Translating club, player, or competition **names**.
   517	- Localised audio, or Bert's voice cues.
   518	- App Store listing metadata and screenshots. Worth doing, separate job.
   519	- Locale-specific currency, date, or calendar systems. The game's week/season
   520	  clock is fictional.
   521	
   522	---
   523	
   524	## 11. Open decisions
   525	
   526	1. **VT323 vs Handjet** for Vietnamese — resolved by the Phase 0 visual gate.
   527	2. **`vars()` vs family-name aliasing** for the font swap — resolved by the
   528	   Phase 0 native spike.
   529	3. **Whether the first-launch picker suggests the device language.** Spec says
   530	   scroll-into-view but do not auto-apply; this is reversible and cheap either
   531	   way.

