   176	Missing key in a non-English catalog falls back to English rather than showing
   177	the raw key. In development it also warns, and CI treats a missing key as a
   178	failure (§8).
   179	
   180	### 3.3 Content JSON, and the single source of English
   181	
   182	`content/*.json` carries ~1,270 translatable prose strings — events, tips,
   183	glossary, ceremony lines, coach reactions. These already have stable ids, so a
   184	translation is keyed off that id (`event.derby-night.body`) rather than
   185	duplicating the whole file per locale. `content/clubs.json` is club and player
   186	**names** and is not translated.
   187	
   188	**There must be exactly one English source, or the two will drift.** Content
   189	prose keeps living in `content/*.json` where it is authored today; the English
   190	catalog does **not** duplicate it by hand. Concretely:
   191	
   192	- `content/i18n/en.json` is **generated, never hand-edited.** A build script
   193	  extracts it from the two English sources — the content files (by id) and the
   194	  keyed strings lifted out of TS/TSX — and writes the merged catalog. It carries
   195	  a `DO NOT EDIT` header and CI fails if regenerating it produces a diff.
   196	- The six translated catalogs **are** hand-authored, against the generated
   197	  English.
   198	- So editing English copy means editing `content/*.json` or the call site, never
   199	  `en.json`. That keeps one place to change a sentence and makes "the English
   200	  changed, these translations are now stale" a mechanical diff (§9.2).
   201	
   202	Without this rule, a writer fixes a tip in `tips.json`, the catalog still holds
   203	the old English, and every translation is silently reviewed against text the
   204	game no longer shows.
   205	
   206	### 3.4 Number formatting
   207	
   208	The same Hermes caution that rules out `Intl.PluralRules` (§3.1) rules out
   209	`Intl.NumberFormat`. The formatting helper is **hand-rolled**: group digits in
   210	threes and pick the separator from a seven-entry table (`.` for `de`, `es`,
   211	`pt-BR`, `id`, `vi`; `,` for `en`; thin space for `fr`). Seven locales do not
   212	justify a polyfill, and a hand-rolled grouper is trivially unit-testable in the
   213	node Jest environment.
   214	
   215	This replaces the mix of `toLocaleString('en-US')`, `toLocaleString('en-GB')`
   216	(`player-request-view-model.ts:111`), and unpinned `toLocaleString()`
   217	(`view-models.ts:560`, `event-selection.ts:189`, `store.ts` ×4) — the last of
   218	which already varies by *device* locale today and is a live bug regardless of
   219	this project.
   220	
   221	Formatting happens at the UI edge only, on the raw values in `labelParams`
   222	(§4.3). It must never run inside a match tick or anywhere its output could
   223	reach a deterministic log.
   224	
   225	---
   226	
   227	## 4. The three hard constraints
   228	
   229	### 4.1 Vietnamese does not render in the game's font
   230	
   231	Measured from the shipped TTF: Silkscreen maps **226 glyphs**, Latin-1 plus a
   232	handful of punctuation. Every Vietnamese letter with a stacked or below-base
   233	diacritic is absent — `ế ộ ữ ạ ằ ọ đ ơ ư` and ~125 more. The combining marks
   234	Vietnamese needs (`U+0323` dot below, `U+0309` hook above, `U+031B` horn) are
   235	absent too, so even composed rendering fails.
   236	
   237	iOS would silently substitute the system face **per glyph**, producing words
   238	that switch typeface mid-syllable. Unacceptable.
   239	
   240	**Chosen approach: a second pixel face for Vietnamese only.** Two Google Fonts
   241	were verified to cover the full Vietnamese subset by parsing their `cmap`:
   242	
   243	| Font | Glyphs | Weights shipped | Also covers | Look |
   244	| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
   245	| VT323 | 568 | **400 only** | Turkish | Solid-stroke bitmap terminal, monospace |
   246	| **Handjet** | 1322 | **400 + 700** (100–900 available) | Turkish, Cyrillic, Greek | Dot-matrix, variable |
   247	
   248	**Recommendation: Handjet**, and the reason is structural rather than aesthetic.
   249	`PixelText.tsx` defines two type voices — `display` is Silkscreen **Bold**,
   250	`data` is Silkscreen **Regular** — and states outright that a bold request on
   251	the regular cut "produces synthetic faux-bold, which smears a 1-bit bitmap
   252	font." VT323 ships a single weight, so choosing it would collapse both voices
   253	into one face for Vietnamese alone and silently delete a distinction the art
   254	bible treats as load-bearing. `@expo-google-fonts/handjet` ships
   255	`Handjet_400Regular` and `Handjet_700Bold` as static cuts, mirroring Silkscreen
   256	one-for-one, and static cuts sidestep React Native's poor variable-axis support.
   257	
   258	Handjet's Cyrillic and Greek coverage is free headroom if a future language
   259	wants them.
   260	
   261	**Consequence for §4.2: Handjet is not monospace.** An earlier draft leaned on
   262	VT323 being monospace to collapse Vietnamese to a single advance constant. That
