   591	
   592	## 7. Phasing
   593	
   594	### 7.1 Enabled locales, not "all locales"
   595	
   596	A language is either **enabled** or it is not shipped. `ENABLED_LOCALES` is a
   597	single constant; the picker (§5) lists exactly what is in it, and every CI
   598	quality gate runs against exactly what is in it. An unenabled locale may hold a
   599	partial catalog with no consequence.
   600	
   601	This exists because the alternative is broken. Gate 1 ("every English key exists
   602	in all six catalogs") plus a phase plan where Spanish lands before German means
   603	either CI blocks every partial ship, or five catalogs get stuffed with English
   604	stubs that pass the presence check and then fail the budget and glyph checks as
   605	noise. Enabled-locale gating is what makes phasing and CI able to coexist.
   606	
   607	### 7.2 A phase is a playable stop, not a coat of paint
   608	
   609	An earlier draft claimed "nothing is left half-English at a stopping point."
   610	That was false: translating the chrome while ~1,270 content strings stayed
   611	English would leave a Spanish player reading English event prose in their first
   612	session. The honest version of the promise:
   613	
   614	> **A stopping point is coherent for a full play session, in the order a player
   615	> meets the game.** Chrome and narrative move together, not chrome first.
   616	
   617	Concretely, that pulls the *first-session* narrative forward out of the long
   618	tail: `onboarding.json`, the first-match and early-season entries in
   619	`events.json`, and Bert's critical-path lines in `assistant-guide.json` ship
   620	with Phase 2, not Phase 5. What stays in the long tail is genuinely long-tail —
   621	late-career events, the full coach-reaction pool, the glossary, blame lines.
   622	
   623	### 7.3 The phases
   624	
   625	**Phase 0 — decide the Vietnamese face.** Render Vietnamese in Handjet (400 and
   626	700) and VT323 at the game's real sizes and pick one (§11 decision 1). Handjet
   627	is the recommendation; the check is aesthetic, not structural. The `vars()`
   628	mechanism no longer needs a spike (§4.1); it needs a device smoke test, which
   629	rides along in Phase 1.
   630	
   631	**Phase 1 — plumbing, English only.** Catalog schema, the `en.json` generator,
   632	`useCopy()`, the hand-rolled formatter (§3.4), the `language` preference, the
   633	picker on both screens, the CI gates, and the extraction itself. At the end the
   634	game looks and behaves identically but every string flows through the lookup.
   635	This is the phase that must not regress anything, so its exit criteria are hard:
   636	
   637	- `npx tsc --noEmit` and the full Jest suite pass, including the golden replay.
   638	- **An English catalog snapshot test.** Every key→English pair is committed as a
   639	  snapshot, so a later keying pass cannot silently reword copy. Regenerating
   640	  `en.json` must produce no diff.
   641	- Gate 6 (no hardcoded prose) reports zero violations outside the exempt paths.
   642	- Device pass over the five length-sensitive screens (§8) in English, confirming
   643	  nothing moved.
   644	- **The 13 raw-`fontFamily` files (§4.1) converted to `useFaces()`**, and the
   645	  device smoke test confirming a locale switch changes the face on the match
   646	  screen — not just on className-styled chrome.
   647	- **Explicit `id` fields added to event outcomes** in `content/events.json`
   648	  (§4.3), before any outcome is translated.
   649	- `CFBundleLocalizations` set in `app.json` (§10.1), so the one native rebuild
   650	  this project needs happens here.
   651	
   652	**Phase 2 — Spanish end-to-end, plus the layout work.** The cost probe. Includes
   653	the §4.2 hard dependencies — per-locale `col.*` short forms and extended advance
   654	maps — because they block any non-English header. Includes the first-session
   655	narrative per §7.2. Spanish because it is the highest-value target with a
   656	middling expansion factor: if Spanish fits, French and Portuguese will.
   657	
   658	**Go/no-go after Phase 2.** Measure actual words, actual review cost, and actual
   659	layout breakage against the §6 estimate. If Spanish costs materially more than a
   660	sixth of the budget, the scope conversation happens here — reduce the language
   661	set, or reduce the quality machinery — not after five more languages are half
   662	done.
   663	
   664	**Phase 3 — Vietnamese.** Third, not last, because it is the one that can fail
   665	on font grounds, and failing early is cheaper. Handjet has different advances
   666	from Silkscreen, so **every advance-mapped width shifts for `vi` alone** and
   667	`LEAGUE_COLUMN_WIDTH` widens to the max across enabled locales (§4.2);
   668	re-measurement is critical path here, not a follow-up.
   669	
   670	**Phase 4 — Portuguese, French, German, Indonesian.** German last within the
   671	group; it is the longest and will surface any remaining layout ceilings.
   672	
   673	**Phase 5 — long tail.** Late-career events, the full coach and blame pools,
   674	glossary, remaining ceremony lines. Largest word count, lowest per-string risk.
   675	
   676	Within each translation phase, work goes screen by screen so a partial phase is
   677	still coherent.
   678	
   679	**Every translation phase is: lock the glossary (§9.1) → translate → audit
   680	(§9.2) → in-context spot-check (§9.4).** A phase is not done when the strings
   681	exist; it is done when every string in it carries an `ok` verdict.
   682	
   683	---
   684	
   685	## 8. Testing and CI
   686	
   687	The balance harness and golden-replay conventions already establish that this
   688	project gates on assertions rather than eyeballs. Localisation gets the same —
   689	but only where a gate can actually be enforced. Three of the eight below were
   690	over-specified in an earlier draft and are corrected here.
   691	
   692	**Where they run.** Jest is `testEnvironment: node` with `roots: ['<rootDir>/src']`
   693	and no jsdom, and `require('react-native')` throws. Gates 1–5, 7 and 8 are pure
   694	data checks — catalog JSON, TTF `cmap` parsing, arithmetic — so they live in
   695	`src/i18n/__tests__/` and reach the catalogs with `fs.readFileSync` on
   696	`content/i18n/`. The `roots` setting limits *test discovery*, not what a test may
   697	read. Gate 6 is not a Jest concern at all.
   698	
   699	All locale-scoped gates run against `ENABLED_LOCALES` only (§7.1).
   700	
   701	1. **No missing keys.** Every key a call site can request exists in the English
   702	   catalog, and every English key exists in every *enabled* locale.
   703	2. **No orphan keys — softened, and here is why.** A pure static reference graph
   704	   cannot see `` t(`event.${id}.body`) ``, so hard-failing on "nothing
   705	   references this key" would either flag every live content key as dead or push
   706	   the API toward literal-only calls. Instead: a catalog key is legal if it is
   707	   either statically referenced **or** matches a prefix in a declared
   708	   **dynamic-key registry** — `event.<id>.*`, `bert.<id>.*`, `tip.<id>.*`, and
   709	   so on, whose legal ids are generated from `content/*.json` itself. A key
   710	   matching neither is the failure. Until that registry exists (Phase 1), this
   711	   gate is a **warning**, not a build break.
   712	3. **Character budget.** Every string within its §1 ceiling. This is a
   713	   *succinctness* gate. It is explicitly **not** a layout proof — see §4.2.
   714	4. **Placeholder parity.** A translated string uses exactly the placeholders its
   715	   English source uses. A dropped `{player}` is a silent content bug.
   716	5. **Glyph coverage.** Every character in a locale's catalog exists in that
   717	   locale's font, checked against the TTF `cmap` — the technique already used
   718	   for column advances. Catches a stray `ı`, a smart quote, or an em dash that
   719	   Silkscreen does not have.
   720	6. **No hardcoded prose — an ESLint job, not a Jest test.** A rule over
   721	   `src/ui/`, **`src/render/`**, `src/application/` and `App.tsx` rejecting JSX
   722	   text nodes and prose literals outside the catalog. It needs TSX AST access
   723	   that the node Jest suite cannot provide, so it runs as its own CI step.
   724	   `src/ui/dev-harness/`, `src/audit/` and developer-mode strings are exempt and
   725	   stay English.
   726	
   727	   `src/render/` is called out because an earlier draft omitted it while §6's
   728	   own table counts 358 strings across 59 render-ring files. Left out, the
   729	   substitution board, power takeovers and coaching modals could keep hardcoded
   730	   English forever and still pass CI.
   731	
   732	   **The rule must also cover accessibility props.** There are **343**
   733	   `accessibilityLabel` / `accessibilityHint` strings outside tests and the dev
   734	   harness. They are props, not JSX text nodes, so a naive rule is structurally
   735	   blind to them — and the result is VoiceOver reading English to players in six
   736	   languages. They are in scope, and they count toward the §6 totals.
   737	7. **Golden replay still matches, or the bump is deliberate.** The earlier
   738	   wording — "localisation must not touch `ENGINE_VERSION`" — was a prohibition
   739	   dressed as a test, and prohibitions do not catch anything. The real gate is
   740	   the existing golden-replay snapshot: it either matches or it does not. If
   741	   extraction changes control flow or RNG consumption (building a label that
   742	   sized an array, branching on English text), the snapshot fails and that
   743	   forces the version decision CLAUDE.md already requires. Expected outcome is
   744	   no bump; the gate is what proves it rather than assuming it.
   745	8. **Width tables per locale.** Every `col.*` string in every enabled locale has
   746	   a measured advance **and** fits its column, for that locale's face. This is
   747	   the gate that makes §4.2 real rather than remembered.
   748	
   749	Device QA per language: onboarding through first match, the league table, the
   750	squad register, the financial report, and Settings — the five screens where
   751	length and glyph coverage bite.
   752	
   753	---
   754	
   755	## 9. Translation quality
   756	
   757	The gates in §8 prove a translation is *present, sized, and renderable*. None of
   758	them prove it is *good*. A string can pass every check and still read like a
   759	manual, invert a meaning, or call a goalkeeper two different things on two
   760	screens.
   761	
   762	**The council pushed back hard on this section, and the pushback was fair.** The
   763	objection: an LLM asked to review another LLM's translation will rubber-stamp
   764	fluent-but-wrong copy, and recording an `ok` verdict against a source hash
   765	proves *coverage*, not *correctness* — paperwork with a green tick. That is a
   766	real failure mode and the previous draft did not answer it.
   767	
   768	The answer is not to delete the audit — auditing every string once is a stated
   769	requirement — but to (a) measure whether the reviewer is actually catching
   770	anything, (b) stop treating the weakest checks as merge blockers, and (c) be
   771	plain about what the whole thing is worth.
   772	
   773	### 9.1 Locked terminology — the strongest lever, and the one that really works
   774	
   775	Consistency across ~2,500 strings cannot be recovered after the fact. Before any
   776	phase-2+ translation, each enabled locale gets a glossary at
   777	`content/i18n/glossary/<locale>.json`.
   778	
   779	**Scoped to coined terms, not to football vocabulary at large.** The game's own
   780	nouns — Heat, the Zone, Hero License, Awakening, Training Points, Fan Shop,
   781	Buzz, Superpower — are what players learn and what must never wobble. Roughly
   782	20 entries, not 80.
   783	
   784	Ordinary football words (goalkeeper, clean sheet, matchday) stay *advisory*: a
   785	recommended rendering recorded for the translator, not a CI assertion. A
   786	substring check on those would fail constantly on Spanish and German inflection
   787	and compounding — `Innenverteidiger` inside `Innenverteidigerposition`,
   788	`portero` versus `porteros` — and a gate that cries wolf gets switched off.
   789	
   790	Each coined term declares its **allowed surface forms** (including inflections)
   791	per locale, and CI fails a string that renders a coined term any other way. That
   792	is a narrow, unambiguous, genuinely enforceable check.
   793	
   794	### 9.2 Every string is audited once, by a different reviewer
   795	
   796	Each translated string is reviewed by an independent reviewer — a different
   797	model from the one that authored it, prompted as a native speaker of that
   798	language who follows football and plays management games, given the target
   799	string, the English source, the glossary, and the screen the string appears on.
   800	
   801	| Verdict | Meaning | Action |
   802	| --- | --- | --- |
   803	| `ok` | Ships as written | none |
   804	| `stiff` | Correct but reads written, not spoken | rewrite, re-review |
   805	| `wrong` | Meaning changed, term off-glossary, placeholder misused | rewrite, re-review |
   806	| `long` | Correct but will not fit its cell | shorten in-language, re-review |
   807	
   808	Verdicts land in `content/i18n/review/<locale>.json` with the reviewer's
   809	one-line reason and the **hash of the English source** they judged. When English
   810	copy changes, exactly the affected strings go stale rather than the whole
   811	language.
   812	
   813	**Coverage is a gate: no enabled locale ships with a string lacking an `ok`
   814	verdict against the current English hash.** That is what makes "audited at least
   815	once" enforceable. It is a coverage gate and it is labelled as one — it asserts
   816	the review happened, not that the copy is good.
   817	
   818	### 9.3 Canaries — the check that makes 9.2 more than paperwork
   819	
   820	This is the answer to "the reviewer will just rubber-stamp it."
   821	
   822	Each review batch is seeded with **canaries**: strings deliberately broken in
   823	ways the reviewer is supposed to catch — a negation dropped, a coined term
   824	swapped for an off-glossary synonym, register flipped to formal (`usted`, `Sie`,
   825	`Anda`), a placeholder removed, a literal calque no native speaker would say.
   826	They are shuffled in and indistinguishable from real work.
   827	
   828	The reviewer never learns which are canaries. Afterwards:
   829	
   830	- **A canary marked `ok` is a miss.** Miss rate is computed per batch and
   831	  recorded next to the verdicts.
   832	- **A batch missing more than 20% of its canaries is void.** Its `ok` verdicts
   833	  are discarded and the batch is re-reviewed with a different reviewer or a
   834	  sharper prompt.
   835	
   836	This converts an unmeasurable worry into a measured false-negative rate. If the
   837	rate is bad, the number says so and the spec's own claim about the audit
   838	collapses honestly instead of quietly. Roughly 5% canaries per batch is enough
   839	to be statistically useful without much cost.
   840	
   841	### 9.4 Register spot-check, in context
   842	
   843	Twenty strings per language, sampled across the screens where voice matters most
   844	— Bert's lines, button labels, the post-match report, event prose — reviewed
   845	**on a screenshot of the actual screen**, not in a list. Out of context, "Free"
   846	is a fine translation of "Free"; on the transfer screen it means "available",
   847	and only the screenshot shows that.
   848	
   849	This catches what the automated gates structurally cannot, and it is small
   850	enough to actually happen for all six languages.
   851	
   852	### 9.5 Back-translation — tooling, demoted from a gate
   853	
   854	A blind back-translation (reviewer sees only the translated string, writes what
   855	it means in English, diff against the source) does catch inversions and dropped
   856	negations. But as a whole-catalog merge blocker over ~15,000 strings it is
   857	expensive, it thrashes on short UI chrome where "Save" back-translates a dozen
   858	defensible ways, and a model that mistranslated a string will often
   859	back-translate its own error into something that diffs clean.
   860	
   861	So it runs **on long prose only** — `event.*`, `bert.*`, tips, ceremony lines —
   862	where the signal is real and the string is long enough for a diff to mean
   863	something. It produces a triage list, not a build failure. Short chrome is
   864	covered by §9.2 plus placeholder parity.
   865	
   866	### 9.6 What this is worth
   867	
   868	Being plain, because the previous draft was not: this is a layered machine
   869	audit, not a native-speaker sign-off. Ranked by how much they actually buy:
   870	
   871	1. **Locked coined terms** (§9.1) — genuinely enforceable, catches the most
   872	   player-visible class of error.
   873	2. **Placeholder parity, glyph coverage, width tables** (§8) — structural, and
   874	   they either pass or they do not.
   875	3. **Canary-validated per-string review** (§9.2–9.3) — worth what the measured
   876	   miss rate says it is worth, and no more. That number gets reported.
   877	4. **In-context spot-check** (§9.4) — small, high-yield, honest about its scale.
   878	5. **Back-translation on prose** (§9.5) — useful triage, weakest evidence.
   879	
   880	It will not catch every regionalism, and it should not be described to anyone as
   881	equivalent to the balance harness — that gate measures a closed system, this one
   882	estimates a human judgement. The plan is to ship, then fix on player reports,
   883	which the catalog format makes a one-line data change rather than a code edit.
   884	
   885	---
   886	
   887	## 10. Out of scope
   888	
   889	- Arabic, Chinese, and any other non-Latin script. Named in §2 with reasons.
   890	- Right-to-left layout support.
   891	- Translating club, player, or competition **names**.
   892	- Localised audio, or Bert's voice cues.
   893	- App Store listing metadata and screenshots. Worth doing, separate job.
   894	- Locale-specific currency, date, or calendar systems. The game's week/season
   895	  clock is fictional.
   896	
   897	### 10.1 Two native changes that are IN scope, and cost a rebuild
   898	
   899	Both are easy to mistake for store metadata. Neither is.
   900	
   901	- **`CFBundleLocalizations` in `app.json`.** Without it iOS reports the app as
   902	  English-only and the App Store page does not list the seven languages, however
   903	  well the in-app picker works. This is app config, not listing copy.
   904	- **`expo-localization`, if the picker suggests the device language.** It is not
   905	  in `package.json` today, so adding it is a new native dependency — which per
   906	  this project's own phone-dev rules means a rebuild, not a Metro reload. If
   907	  Open Decision 3 lands on "do not suggest", this dependency is not needed at
   908	  all; that is a point in favour of not suggesting.
   909	
   910	Both land in Phase 1 so the single rebuild covers them together.
   911	
   912	---
   913	
   914	## 11. Open decisions
   915	
   916	1. **VT323 vs Handjet** for Vietnamese — **Handjet recommended**, on structural
   917	   grounds: it ships 400 and 700 cuts and so preserves the game's two type
   918	   voices, where VT323's single weight would collapse them (§4.1). What remains
   919	   open is purely aesthetic — Handjet's dots versus Silkscreen's solid blocks —
   920	   and Phase 0 settles it by rendering one real Vietnamese screen. Choosing
   921	   VT323 anyway means consciously accepting one weight for Vietnamese.
   922	2. ~~`vars()` vs family-name aliasing~~ — **closed.** `vars()` is verified
   923	   working on native by source-tracing `react-native-css-interop@0.2.6` (§4.1).
   924	   Family-name aliasing is rejected as a product path, not held as a fallback:
   925	   it forces a relaunch on language change, which contradicts the live switch
   926	   §5 promises. If the Phase 1 device smoke test somehow fails, the thing that
   927	   changes is §5's promise — and that is a decision to bring back to Joe, not a
   928	   silent downgrade.
   929	3. **Whether the first-launch picker suggests the device language.** Spec says
   930	   scroll-into-view but do not auto-apply; reversible and cheap either way.
   931	4. **Whether Phase 5's long tail gets the full §9 quality machine** or a lighter
   932	   pass. Deliberately deferred to the post-Phase-2 go/no-go (§7.3), when the
   933	   measured cost of one language exists and the decision can be made on a
   934	   number instead of a guess.
   470	    <SafeAreaProvider>
   471	      {!fontsLoaded ? <LoadingScreen /> : (
   472	        <>
   473	          <StatusBar style="light" />
   474	          <DevHarnessScreen />
   475	        </>
   476	      )}
   477	    </SafeAreaProvider>
   478	  );
   479	}
