    93	## 3. Where translation happens
    94	
    95	**At the UI edge, never in the pure rings.**
    96	
    97	`src/sim/` and `src/game/` stay pure TypeScript and stay deterministic. Today
    98	some of them emit English sentences — `src/game/career.ts` builds ledger labels
    99	like `'Fan Shop merchandise'`, and `src/application/view-models.ts` builds
   100	`` `Season ${state.season}` ``. Those become **keys plus parameters**:
   101	
   102	```ts
   103	// before
   104	{ label: 'Fan Shop merchandise', amount }
   105	// after
   106	{ labelKey: 'ledger.merch', labelParams: {}, amount }
   107	```
   108	
   109	The UI resolves the key. This is required by the architecture rule (no
   110	locale-dependent behaviour in a ring that has to produce byte-identical results
   111	from a seed), and it is what makes a saved career render in whatever language
   112	the player later switches to.
   113	
   114	**The three layers that emit English, and what each becomes:**
   115	
   116	| Layer | Examples | Becomes |
   117	| --- | --- | --- |
   118	| Pure sim (`src/sim/`) | `tactics.ts:46–50` formation blurbs — `'Balanced lines'`, `'Wide attack'`, `'Midfield shield'`, `'Deep counter'`, `'Crowd midfield'` | Display-only maps; the ring keeps `FormationId` and the blurb map moves to the catalog. No behaviour changes, so no `ENGINE_VERSION` bump — but see §8 gate 7 |
   119	| Pure game (`src/game/`) | `career.ts` ledger labels, `management.ts` shortfall sentences, `promotion-progression.ts` drill titles | `labelKey` + `labelParams` |
   120	| Application (`src/application/`) | `store.ts:1482–1776` toasts, `view-models.ts:560` settlement lines, `event-selection.ts:189` requirement lines | `labelKey` + `labelParams` — these are the easiest to forget because they are not "UI" and not "pure ring" |
   121	
   122	That application layer is the one an extraction pass skips by accident. It is
   123	called out here so it gets its own inventory item in the plan rather than being
   124	swept up under "the UI".
   125	
   126	`src/sim/teams.ts` club names stay English — they are names (§10).
   127	
   128	### 3.1 Catalog format
   129	
   130	One file per locale, `content/i18n/<locale>.json`, zod-validated at load like
   131	every other content file:
   132	
   133	```json
   134	{
   135	  "schemaVersion": 1,
   136	  "locale": "de",
   137	  "strings": {
   138	    "creation.stat.pac.label": "TEMPO",
   139	    "creation.stat.pac.detail": "Antritt und Rücklauf",
   140	    "ledger.merch": "Fanshop",
   141	    "market.bid.confirm": "{club} kauft {player} für {fee}"
   142	  }
   143	}
   144	```
   145	
   146	- Keys are dot-namespaced by screen or system: `creation.*`, `match.*`,
   147	  `ledger.*`, `settings.*`, `bert.*`, `event.<id>.*`.
   148	- Interpolation is `{name}` placeholders only. No nested markup, no HTML.
   149	- Plurals use explicit sibling keys (`.one` / `.other`) resolved by an
   150	  `Intl.PluralRules`-free hand-rolled selector — Hermes' `Intl` coverage varies
   151	  by platform and the game must behave the same on both. The six target
   152	  languages need exactly three rules:
   153	
   154	  | Rule | Locales | Behaviour |
   155	  | --- | --- | --- |
   156	  | `n === 1` singular | `es`, `de`, `en` | 0 takes `.other` |
   157	  | `n === 0 \|\| n === 1` singular | `fr`, **`pt-BR`** | 0 takes `.one` |
   158	  | no plural marking | `id`, `vi` | always `.other`, one form |
   159	
   160	  Brazilian Portuguese belongs with French, not with Spanish — CLDR treats
   161	  `pt` as `i = 0 or 1 → one`, so "0 jogadores" is wrong and "0 jogador" is
   162	  right. An earlier draft grouped it as "Germanic/Romance one-vs-other" and
   163	  would have shipped that bug in every zero-count string.
   164	
   165	### 3.2 Runtime
   166	
   167	A `useCopy()` hook backed by a Zustand slice, seeded from the persisted
   168	`language` preference:
   169	
   170	```ts
   171	const t = useCopy();
   172	t('creation.stat.pac.label');           // "TEMPO"
   173	t('market.bid.confirm', { club, player, fee });
   174	```
   175	
   176	Missing key in a non-English catalog falls back to English rather than showing
   177	the raw key. In development it also warns, and CI treats a missing key as a
   178	failure (§8).
   179	
   180	### 3.3 Content JSON, and the single source of English
   181	
   182	`content/*.json` carries ~1,270 translatable prose strings — events, tips,
   183	glossary, ceremony lines, coach reactions. These already have stable ids, so a
   184	translation is keyed off that id (`event.derby-night.body`) rather than
   185	duplicating the whole file per locale. `content/clubs.json` is club and player
   186	**names** and is not translated.
   187	
   188	**There must be exactly one English source, or the two will drift.** Content
   189	prose keeps living in `content/*.json` where it is authored today; the English
   190	catalog does **not** duplicate it by hand. Concretely:
   191	
   192	- `content/i18n/en.json` is **generated, never hand-edited.** A build script
   193	  extracts it from the two English sources — the content files (by id) and the
   194	  keyed strings lifted out of TS/TSX — and writes the merged catalog. It carries
   195	  a `DO NOT EDIT` header and CI fails if regenerating it produces a diff.
   196	- The six translated catalogs **are** hand-authored, against the generated
   197	  English.
   198	- So editing English copy means editing `content/*.json` or the call site, never
   199	  `en.json`. That keeps one place to change a sentence and makes "the English
   200	  changed, these translations are now stale" a mechanical diff (§9.2).
   201	
   202	Without this rule, a writer fixes a tip in `tips.json`, the catalog still holds
   203	the old English, and every translation is silently reviewed against text the
   204	game no longer shows.
   205	
   685	## 8. Testing and CI
   686	
   687	The balance harness and golden-replay conventions already establish that this
   688	project gates on assertions rather than eyeballs. Localisation gets the same —
   689	but only where a gate can actually be enforced. Three of the eight below were
   690	over-specified in an earlier draft and are corrected here.
   691	
   692	**Where they run.** Jest is `testEnvironment: node` with `roots: ['<rootDir>/src']`
   693	and no jsdom, and `require('react-native')` throws. Gates 1–5, 7 and 8 are pure
   694	data checks — catalog JSON, TTF `cmap` parsing, arithmetic — so they live in
   695	`src/i18n/__tests__/` and reach the catalogs with `fs.readFileSync` on
   696	`content/i18n/`. The `roots` setting limits *test discovery*, not what a test may
   697	read. Gate 6 is not a Jest concern at all.
   698	
   699	All locale-scoped gates run against `ENABLED_LOCALES` only (§7.1).
   700	
   701	1. **No missing keys.** Every key a call site can request exists in the English
   702	   catalog, and every English key exists in every *enabled* locale.
   703	2. **No orphan keys — softened, and here is why.** A pure static reference graph
   704	   cannot see `` t(`event.${id}.body`) ``, so hard-failing on "nothing
   705	   references this key" would either flag every live content key as dead or push
   706	   the API toward literal-only calls. Instead: a catalog key is legal if it is
   707	   either statically referenced **or** matches a prefix in a declared
   708	   **dynamic-key registry** — `event.<id>.*`, `bert.<id>.*`, `tip.<id>.*`, and
   709	   so on, whose legal ids are generated from `content/*.json` itself. A key
   710	   matching neither is the failure. Until that registry exists (Phase 1), this
   711	   gate is a **warning**, not a build break.
   712	3. **Character budget.** Every string within its §1 ceiling. This is a
   713	   *succinctness* gate. It is explicitly **not** a layout proof — see §4.2.
   714	4. **Placeholder parity.** A translated string uses exactly the placeholders its
   715	   English source uses. A dropped `{player}` is a silent content bug.
   716	5. **Glyph coverage.** Every character in a locale's catalog exists in that
   717	   locale's font, checked against the TTF `cmap` — the technique already used
   718	   for column advances. Catches a stray `ı`, a smart quote, or an em dash that
   719	   Silkscreen does not have.
   720	6. **No hardcoded prose — an ESLint job, not a Jest test.** A rule over
   721	   `src/ui/`, **`src/render/`**, `src/application/` and `App.tsx` rejecting JSX
   722	   text nodes and prose literals outside the catalog. It needs TSX AST access
   723	   that the node Jest suite cannot provide, so it runs as its own CI step.
   724	   `src/ui/dev-harness/`, `src/audit/` and developer-mode strings are exempt and
   725	   stay English.
   726	
   727	   `src/render/` is called out because an earlier draft omitted it while §6's
   728	   own table counts 358 strings across 59 render-ring files. Left out, the
   729	   substitution board, power takeovers and coaching modals could keep hardcoded
   730	   English forever and still pass CI.
   731	
   732	   **The rule must also cover accessibility props.** There are **343**
   733	   `accessibilityLabel` / `accessibilityHint` strings outside tests and the dev
   734	   harness. They are props, not JSX text nodes, so a naive rule is structurally
   735	   blind to them — and the result is VoiceOver reading English to players in six
   736	   languages. They are in scope, and they count toward the §6 totals.
   737	7. **Golden replay still matches, or the bump is deliberate.** The earlier
   738	   wording — "localisation must not touch `ENGINE_VERSION`" — was a prohibition
   739	   dressed as a test, and prohibitions do not catch anything. The real gate is
   740	   the existing golden-replay snapshot: it either matches or it does not. If
   741	   extraction changes control flow or RNG consumption (building a label that
   742	   sized an array, branching on English text), the snapshot fails and that
   743	   forces the version decision CLAUDE.md already requires. Expected outcome is
   744	   no bump; the gate is what proves it rather than assuming it.
   745	8. **Width tables per locale.** Every `col.*` string in every enabled locale has
   746	   a measured advance **and** fits its column, for that locale's face. This is
   747	   the gate that makes §4.2 real rather than remembered.
   748	
   749	Device QA per language: onboarding through first match, the league table, the
   750	squad register, the financial report, and Settings — the five screens where
   751	length and glyph coverage bite.
